{"gudgeon":{"id":"b280f518-ba33-400f-a552-995b236306cc","word":"gudgeon","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"Gobio gobio","tags":["masculine"],"word":"gobião"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"Gobio gobio","tags":["masculine"],"word":"góbio"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"[...] Ile tell thee more of this another time. / But fiſh not with this melancholy baite, / For this foole gudgin, this opinion: [...]","ref":"c. 1596–1598 (date written), W[illiam] Shakespeare, The Excellent History of the Merchant of Venice. […] (First Quarto), [London]: […] J[ames] Roberts [for Thomas Heyes], published 1600, →OCLC, [Act I, scene i]:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[107,113]],"id":"cb96812f-263a-467f-96cc-24ad69b7c45d"},{"text":"[...] Make Fools believe in their fore-seeing / Of things before they are in Being; / To swallow Gudgeons ere th' are catch'd, / And count their Chickens ere th' are hatch'd, [...]","ref":"1663 (indicated as 1664), [Samuel Butler], “The Second Part of Hudibras. Canto III.”, in Hudibras. The First and Second Parts. […], London: […] John Martyn and Henry Herringman, […], published 1678, →OCLC; republished in A[lfred] R[ayney] Waller, editor, Hudibras: Written in the Time of the Late Wars, Cambridge, Cambridgeshire: University Press, 1905, →OCLC, page 175:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[97,105]],"id":"3fb47d01-e7af-42b1-9497-0bf632cc8bd9"},{"text":"The Gudgeon is reputed a fish of excellent taste, and to be very wholesome: he is of a fine shape, of a silver colour, and beautified with black spots both on his body and tail.","ref":"1676, Izaak Walton, “[The Compleat Angler or The Contemplative Man’s Recreation: Part I […].] Observations of the Gudgeon, the Ruffe, and the Bleak, and How to Fish for Them.”, in Richard Le Gallienne, editor, The Compleat Angler, 5th edition, London; New York, N.Y.: John Lane, The Bodley Head, published 1897, →OCLC, page 199:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,11]],"id":"929437d3-afcb-4edd-955a-1f6383a4d433"},{"text":"You save your souls here, eating cabbage, and think you are the righteous. You eat a gudgeon a day, and you think you bribe God with gudgeon.","ref":"1912, Fyodor Dostoevsky, “Why is such a Man Alive?”, in Constance Garnett, transl., The Brothers Karamazov […], New York, N.Y.: The Macmillan Company, published 1922, part I, book II (An Unfortunate Gathering), page 73:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[85,92],[133,140]],"id":"9c2f191e-d0bd-4bca-8477-c58e089170b2"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Entries missing English vernacular names of taxa"],"links":[["small","small"],["freshwater","freshwater#Adjective"],["fish","fish#Noun"],["Gobio gobio","Gobio gobio#Translingual"],["native","native#Adjective"],["Eurasia","Eurasia"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"goby"},{"word":"wapper","tags":["Britain","dialectal"]}],"glosses":["A common gudgeon, a small freshwater fish of species Gobio gobio, that is native to Eurasia."],"id":"5de1cb3c-f045-47b6-a25f-9f5eeb287ba2"},{"categories":["Canadian English","Entries missing English vernacular names of taxa","Entries using missing taxonomic name (species)"],"raw_glosses":["(Canada) Cottus bairdii, more widely known as mottled sculpin."],"glosses":["Cottus bairdii, more widely known as mottled sculpin."],"tags":["Canada"],"id":"0c61a251-f9b5-4880-803b-757146d680a6"},{"examples":[{"text":"The southern purple-spotted gudgeon [Mogurnda adspersa] can be hard to find. Although colourful, they are thin and only grow up to 12 centimetres.","ref":"2012 October 5, Nicola Gage, “Murray Showing Signs of Recovery”, in ABC News, Sydney, N.S.W., archived from the original on 01 Nov 2019:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[28,35]],"id":"49087c78-ad42-4dd4-9e09-6c0b1ca30735"}],"categories":["Australian English","English terms with quotations"],"links":[["similar","similar"],["Eleotridae","Eleotridae#Translingual"],["used","use#Verb"],["bait","bait#Noun"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"sleeper goby"}],"raw_glosses":["(Australia) Any of various similar small fish of the family Eleotridae, often used as bait."],"glosses":["Any of various similar small fish of the family Eleotridae, often used as bait."],"tags":["Australia"],"id":"089c2088-b410-42a5-a77a-52b406064963"},{"categories":["Entries using missing taxonomic name (family)"],"links":[["Eleotridae","Eleotridae#Translingual"]],"glosses":["Other fish, similar in appearance, principally in families Butidae and Eleotridae, but also in others."],"id":"5173b82d-dcc0-4828-8616-ac96f60cdeeb"},{"examples":[{"text":"The Doctor now obeys the Summons, / Likes both his Company, and Commons; / Diſplays his Talent, ſits till Ten; / Next Day invited, comes again; / Soon grows Domeſtick, ſeldom fails, / Either at Morning, or at Meals; / Came early, and departed late: / In ſhort, the Gudgeon took the Bait.","ref":"1713, Jonathan Swift, “Horace, Epistle VII. Book I. Imitated and Addressed to the Earl of Oxford, in the Year 1713”, in Miscellanies. The Last Volume, London: […] Benjamin Motte, […], published 1733, →OCLC, page 149, lines 73–80:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[265,272]],"id":"86d9bb55-52f5-4a42-a96c-e21783138dcf"},{"text":"\"Is it not also known that you two innocents were doing the Churches week by week? Was it not patent that sooner or later you would come to a Spiritualist gathering? Here was a chance for a convert! They set a bait and poor old gudgeon Malone came along and swallowed it.\"","ref":"1925 July – 1926 May, A[rthur] Conan Doyle, “(please specify the chapter number)”, in The Land of Mist (eBook no. 0601351h.html), Australia: Project Gutenberg Australia, published April 2019:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[228,235]],"id":"456b0b79-0742-4ed9-a4f7-63b4e9311c99"},{"text":"\"Don't be a gudgeon,\" she grumbled, tugging at the dress much more fiercely than was necessary. \"If this works as you say it will, nobody will notice 